//? Title: CondenseNet: Tensor Pruning, Packed Groups and Explicit Optimization
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Learned group convolutions select reusable dense features before conversion into grouped filters.
We inspect tensor mask updates, verify packed values and every derivative, then fit the complete
42-layer dense body with a visible Adam, group-lasso and pruning loop.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.4_efficient_and_mobile_architectures/condensenet.cpp`.

> The method

The CIFAR-86 configuration has three dense stages with 14 layers each and growth 8/16/32.
A 16-channel stem precedes BN/ReLU, a learned 1×1 bottleneck with 4k outputs, then
BN/ReLU and a 3×3 four-group convolution adding k new channels by concatenation.
Transitions average-pool without compression. Final BN/ReLU, global mean and a biased
10-class classifier use all 800 final channels. The full training form has 1451594 learned
scalars and 1273850 BN/mask/stage scalars before its scalar step counter is created.

For C=4 condensation and G=4 output groups, outputs kG+g share a mask for group g.
Importance is the sum of absolute masked weights over that group's outputs. At each of
three stages, the least important Cin/C still-live inputs are removed per group. Stable
ranking resolves equal scores by input index; jumping forward in progress catches up every
missing stage, and moving backwards never resurrects a connection. All production ranking,
mask updates and packed kernel gathering use tensors, preserving Float32/Float64 types.
The host sort below exists only as an independent oracle.

$$L=L_{\mathrm{NLL}}+\lambda\sum_{\ell,i,g}\sqrt{\max(\sum_k(W_{\ell,i,kG+g}M_{\ell,i,g})^2,10^{-6})}
+\frac{10^{-4}}2\sum_{W\text{ kernels}}\lVert W\rVert_2^2.$$
\alttext(The optimized loss combines cross entropy, group lasso on live learned-group weights, and kernel L2.)

Each forward clones its current mask and computes its loss with that snapshot. It then updates
stored masks using PRE-optimizer weights for the NEXT forward. Progress is the pre-forward
step divided by total steps, with target stage min(C−1,floor(2(C−1)progress)). In 18 updates,
the forward at step 9 (the 10th update) stores stage three; the next forward first uses it.
Lasso is included while the snapshot's old stage is below three. BN uses epsilon 1e-5;
learned affine state and moving moments are separate. Frozen diagnostic state disables both
BN and mask/step updates. Training snapshots include parameters, BN, masks, stages and step.
Pruned entries remain dense parameters; L2 and Adam may still change them behind zero masks.

Packing gathers each group's surviving inputs and corresponding BN vectors, packs its kernel,
uses a core grouped convolution, and restores the original interleaved output channel order
with PaperChannelShuffle. The Float64 fixture checks outputs and ALL input/filter/BN derivatives.
This is a converted single-layer demonstration. The separate converted-network cost helper
is ANALYTICAL: its default additionally assumes a half-width final classifier. No fitted
classifier feature-selection algorithm or fully converted network is claimed. Keeping all 800
features costs 4000 extra parameters and MACs in the CIFAR budget. MACs exclude BN, pooling,
activations, gathers and memory traffic; dense masked training does not realize packed speedups.

The visible subset fit retains all stage depths and widths. It uses 48 fit, 32 validation and
32 official-test images; 3 batch-eight epochs (18 updates) of Adam(.001), explicit lasso and kernel L2,
with padded crops/flips. Inference restores the lowest validation-NLL checkpoint with ALL state,
which can precede full condensation. Its saved stages are printed separately from final training
stages. This deliberately tiny single-seed CPU run (kept short so the report builds in seconds)
shows the mechanics only; it does not reproduce paper accuracy or a mobile latency study.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/condensenet_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/condensenet_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/condensenet_concept.tikz > build/condensenet_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/condensenet_concept.svg build/condensenet_concept.dvi > build/condensenet_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
