\caption(Explicit 3-epoch Adam fit and a tensor-computed true/predicted-class count matrix at the selected checkpoint. Training uses augmentation and batch BN moments; validation/test use saved moving moments. Counts sum to 32; IDs follow the original CIFAR-10 order.)
\alttext(Explicit 3-epoch Adam fit and a tensor-computed true/predicted-class count matrix at the selected checkpoint. Training uses augmentation and batch BN moments; validation/test use saved moving moments. Counts sum to 32; IDs follow the original CIFAR-10 order.)


> Observed subset result

The selected epoch 1 checkpoint has validation NLL 2.301555, inference training NLL
2.162721 and official-test NLL 2.338217, with 2/32 correct predictions (6.25%), i.e. no
better than chance: three epochs on 48 images are far too few to learn CIFAR-10 classes.
All 18 training updates ran, and the final masks reached stage three, but the selected
step-6 checkpoint still has stage-one masks, illustrating that the lowest-validation
checkpoint can precede full condensation; validation NLL does not improve after epoch 1
in this tiny experiment. The packed
equivalence check and analytical budgets do not establish that this fitted dense model
achieves mobile inference speedups.

> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

\image(figures/condensenet_confusion_api_0.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 1. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)


> Conclusion

Learned masks prune feature reuse while a packed grouped layer preserves the masked function
and derivatives. Explicit optimization exposes group lasso, the next-forward pruning schedule
and stateful checkpoint selection. Analytical conversion budgets remain separate from the
executed single-layer packing and dense subset fit.

> References

- [Huang et al. (2018), CondenseNet: An Efficient DenseNet using Learned Group Convolutions](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.09224).
- [Released CondenseNet implementation](https://github.com/ShichenLiu/CondenseNet).
- [CIFAR-10 dataset](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html).
